+# CoopDX Metal Integration Plan
+
+## Baseline and scope
+
+- Base: CoopDX iOS `main`, commit `6bfcfede7a5c1af400daa269931a9ae13ed157ad`.
+- Integration branch: `mission-02-metal-integration`, based on the Mission 01 local checkpoint `34e638ec4a9098dda878998a46333ef21fea731c`.
+- Keep CoopDX's display-list interpreter, game loop, Lua, DynOS, SDL/UIKit input and OpenGL ES backend authoritative. Add a renderer behind `GfxRenderingAPI`; do not port the previous game's RSP/display-list engine.
+- Metal remains opt-in until iOS parity and fallback are validated. OpenGL ES remains the recovery backend.
+
+## Existing renderer contract
+
+`gfx_pc.c` interprets display lists, decodes N64 textures, batches triangles into a float VBO, and sends backend operations through `GfxRenderingAPI`. A backend receives generated `ColorCombiner` objects, RGBA texture uploads, two texture slots, sampler modes, alpha/depth/decal state, viewport/scissor, batched triangles, and frame lifecycle callbacks.
+
+Shader creation is semantic, not an integer ID: `ColorCombiner` contains a `CombineMode`, 16 generated shader commands, input mapping and cache hash. The legacy Metal renderer accepts a packed 32-bit ID. Hashing or truncating a CoopDX combiner would lose command ordering, two-cycle behavior, fog/alpha flags, light-map state or input mapping. The integration will instead translate the CoopDX command sequence into an MSL program and cache by a canonical full combiner key.
+
+## Platform presentation
+
+The existing iOS window manager creates an SDL OpenGL window/context and swaps with `SDL_GL_SwapWindow`. Metal requires an SDL Metal-capable view/layer, a drawable acquired per frame, and a Metal command buffer presented at frame completion. Keep the event loop, touch/controller callbacks, orientation and SDL lifecycle, but branch only the surface creation, dimensions, and presentation operations according to the selected backend. The iOS CMake currently defines `RAPI_GL=1`; it must compile both GL and Metal implementations, link Metal/MetalKit/QuartzCore, and keep the existing GL path selectable.
+
+Backend changes require restart. Metal initialization must expose a readiness result to the top-level graphics initialization so a failed device/layer/pipeline setup can shut down Metal and recreate the SDL OpenGL surface before gameplay starts. Never continue with a half-initialized renderer.
+
+## Material and state adapter
+
+The MSL vertex descriptor follows `gfx_opengl_shader_get_info()` semantics: clip-space position, present texture coordinates, optional fog/light-map data, then `num_inputs` generated per-vertex vectors (3 or 4 components depending on `use_alpha`). The Metal backend must not assume model-space positions or native normals: CoopDX's PC renderer transforms vertices before submission.
+
+The fragment adapter evaluates each RGB/alpha combiner group from `shader_commands` using `(A - B) * C + D`, preserving 1-cycle/2-cycle combined values and the upstream optimized cases. It then applies texture-edge discard and fog in the existing order. N64 texture decoding remains in the shared frontend; Metal owns GPU upload/cache, wrap/filter sampler creation, state transitions, depth/decal/blend pipelines and draw submission.
+
+## Reuse and licensing
+
+The inspected CoopDX iOS and `sm64ios-metal` repository roots do not contain a project-level license or copying grant. The Metal project has third-party tool licenses, which do not grant rights to its renderer. Therefore no renderer source is copied in this milestone. Implement the backend and `ColorCombiner` adapter independently against the visible interfaces, and keep the native CoopDX source tree/history intact. Revisit direct reuse only if maintainers provide compatible permission.
+
+## Implementation sequence
+
+1. Build a portable CoopDX-combiner-to-MSL generator with deterministic cache keys and host tests.
+2. Add an iOS-only Metal backend implementing every current `GfxRenderingAPI` callback used by the interpreter.
+3. Add SDL Metal surface/present integration and restart-required backend choice, retaining OpenGL ES fallback.
+4. Validate combiner/state behavior with host tests and Apple Metal shader compilation; build on a macOS runner before claiming iOS compilation.
+5. Exercise vanilla rendering, then DynOS/custom assets and Lua-driven graphics behavior. Defer post-processing and other effects until vanilla parity.
